---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A savings goal calculator answers a simple question: how much to save each month to reach a
      savings target by a certain date. It accounts for what you have already saved and the return
      you expect to earn, then solves for the level monthly savings that get you there on time.
      For example, a $50,000 target in five years is reached with a steady amount each month.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try The Savings Goal Calculator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How It Works <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The calculation is a sinking fund: your current savings grow at the expected return, and
      each monthly contribution also grows for the time remaining. The tool finds the
      contribution that makes the total reach your goal exactly at the deadline.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="time-horizon-and-return">
    <h2>Time Horizon and Return: Shorter Versus Longer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Two inputs move the monthly amount the most. A shorter versus longer time horizon matters
      because a longer timeframe gives compounding more room, so the monthly savings drop.
      Therefore, starting later sharply raises the monthly amount, since the same goal must be
      met with fewer contributions. A higher assumed return lowers the monthly saving but adds
      risk, because reaching for higher returns means more ups and downs along the way.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="examples">
    <h2>Examples <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      <strong>A house down payment.</strong> To reach $50,000 in 5 years with $5,000 already
      saved and a 6% return, you would set aside around $620 a month, and investment growth
      covers several thousand dollars of the goal.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Already there.</strong> If your current savings already exceed the goal, the
      required monthly amount is zero. You can redirect that money to your next goal.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="formula">
    <h2>The Formula <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p><strong>PMT = (FV − P(1 + i)^N) · i / ((1 + i)^N − 1)</strong></p>
    <p>
      where FV is the goal, P is current savings, i is the monthly rate (annual return divided
      by 12), and N is the number of months. When the return is zero, this simplifies to the
      remaining amount divided by the number of months.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Returns reduce your contribution">
      <p>Because your contributions and current balance earn returns along the way, you contribute less than the full goal. The gap between what you put in and the goal is the growth your money earns.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Assuming an unrealistic return">
      <p>
        A high assumed return lowers the monthly figure but increases risk and uncertainty.
        For short goals, keep the rate conservative since there is little time to recover from
        a downturn.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Forgetting current savings">
      <p>
        Include what you have already set aside for the goal. Those savings keep growing and
        count toward the target, which meaningfully reduces the monthly amount you still need.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="Start with the essentials">
      <p>Before saving for discretionary goals, it is wise to have a basic emergency fund so an unexpected cost does not derail your plan. Size that buffer first, then target other goals.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
